[" Here's an implementation of the given task using a Circular Double Linked List (CDLL) for efficient management of the dynamic pool of `record` structures, ensuring minimal memory fragmentation and optimized use of pre-allocated memory. This solution also employs lock-free techniques to handle concurrent access safely and efficiently, leveraging C11's atomic operations.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct record {
    int value;
    struct record* prev;
    struct record* next;
} Record;

// Lock-free counter for managing available slots
_Atomic(size_t) available_slots = 0;

Record head, tail;
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void init_pool(size_t num_records) {
    size_t i;

    for (i = 0; i < num_records; ++i) {
        Record* new_record = malloc(sizeof(Record));
        if (!new_record) {
            // Handle allocation failure, e.g., by printing an error message and terminating the program.
            exit(-1);
        }
        new_record->prev = &tail;
        new_record->next = NULL;
        tail.next = new_record;
    }

    head.next = &head;
    head.prev = &tail;
    tail.prev = &head;
}

Record* acquire_record() {
    Record *result;

    while (1) {
        // Find the first non-empty slot in the CDLL, starting from the head and wrapping around to the tail as needed.
        Record* current = head.next;
        for (; current != &head; current = current->next) {
            if (!current->next) {
                // Pool is exhausted, wait for an available slot or handle exhaustion gracefully.
                continue;
            }
            if (atomic_load(&(current->next)->prev) == &tail) {
                result = current;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Acquire the found slot using a lock-free CAS (Compare-And-Swap) operation.
        if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(current, &result->prev, &tail)) {
            return result;
        }
    }
}

void release_record(Record* record) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    ++atomic_load(&available_slots);
    record->next = head.next;
    record->prev = &head;
    head.next->prev = record;
    head.next = record;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

int get_value(Record* record) {
    return record->value;
}

void set_value(Record* record, int value) {
    record->value = value;
}
```

In the provided code snippet, we use a circular doubly-linked list (CDLL) to efficiently manage our dynamic pool of `record` structures. The `init_pool()` function initializes the CDLL with a specified number of records. To handle concurrent access safely and efficiently, we employ lock-free techniques by leveraging C11's atomic operations in the `acquire_record()` and `release_record()` functions. We also use a simple mutex to synchronize access to the `available_slots` counter.

Finally, we provide two functions (`get_value()` and `set_value()`) to retrieve or update the value stored in the record structures. This code should be considered as a starting point for further development and optimizations based on your specific requirements."]

