```java
@Service
public class TransactionService {
    private final PaymentGateway paymentGateway;
    private final CacheManager cacheManager;
    private final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(TransactionService.class);

    @Autowired
    public TransactionService(PaymentGateway paymentGateway, CacheManager cacheManager) {
        this.paymentGateway = paymentGateway;
        this.cacheManager = cacheManager;
    }

    @Retryable(value = RuntimeException.class, maxAttempts = 3)
    public void processTransaction(Transaction transaction) {
        String uniqueId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        transaction.setUniqueId(uniqueId);

        Object cacheKey = getCacheKey(transaction);
        Object cachedResponse = cacheManager.get(cacheKey);
        if (cachedResponse != null) {
            processCachedResponse(transaction, cachedResponse);
        } else {
            Response response = paymentGateway.processTransaction(transaction);
            validateResponse(response);
            logTransaction(transaction, response);
            cacheManager.put(cacheKey, response);
        }
    }

    // Other methods like getCacheKey(), processCachedResponse(), validateResponse(), logTransaction()
}
```

This example uses Spring's `@Service`, `@Autowired`, and `@Retryable` annotations for service implementation, caching, retries, and error handling. The specifics of each method will depend on the exact requirements of your project and the APIs of the supported payment gateways."]

